    "bounding box" : [ 82.205, 351.74, 513.072, 386.36 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "These estimates can be made contractive if t is chosen small enough, giving local existence. Convergence of (M1(t),M2(t)) to (M1∞,M2∞) as t → ∞ (see (21)) allows to iterate the contraction estimate and implies a global L1-bound of (f1,f2) and therefore global existence."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 82,
    "level" : "5",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 505.322, 338.409, 513.071, 353.014 ],
    "font" : "MSAM10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "□"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 83,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 82.205, 264.615, 513.073, 329.471 ],
    "font" : "CMTI10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Remark 3.1. We notice that the third estimated term, part of the right-hand-side of f2, shows a term with constant proportional to εt. This is a consequence of the fact that the dynamics of f2 are much faster than the ones of f1 and indicates that time-uniform estimates for the fast variable f2 break down for vanishing ε. Clearly the limits ε → 0 and t → 0 do not commute. Thus, we expect difficulties arising taking the the limit ε → 0 for small time t."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 85,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 82.205, 233.491, 513.075, 263.855 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "3.3. Instantaneous limit. We consider the mild formulation (30b) for f2ε which after the coordinate change s  → t − εs reveals its time-delay structure"
  }, {
